 Willowmere village, packing a bag in a great hurry. 'Leaving us?' she asked pleasantly, and the disgraced solicitor's smile did not reach the eyes. Every answer was smooth, and every smooth answer was a lie.\n\nBack in the parlor, Dorothy Lark laid out the evidence: the landlord's tale, the parish gap, the threatening note, the too-perfect description. Constable Henry Bly whistled. 'It's a noose,' they said, 'and the disgraced solicitor tied it.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "They went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a torn letter. The whole sad story lay in the mud.\n\nThe storm that had threatened Willowmere village all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Willowmere village. In the midst of it came a hammering at Dorothy Lark's door: Constable Henry Bly, soaked, with news — the disgraced solicitor had been seen digging near the old vicarage.\n\nThey walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. Constable Henry Bly said nothing, but their silence was agreement. justice in small places — even on the worst night, Dorothy Lark thought, especially then."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "the disgraced solicitor's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Willowmere village was stern, but Willowmere village was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish.\n\nThe constable did his duty, but Dorothy Lark had already done hers: she had made the truth plain, so that no whisper could twist it. Constable Henry Bly squeezed her hand. 'Neatest case I ever saw,' they whispered.\n\nThat evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Dorothy Lark wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. justice in small places — I shall drink tea to that."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': "A week later Willowmere village was itself again: the fete committee argued about bunting, the bakery smelled of scones, and a torn letter sat in its proper place, no longer a mystery but a keepsake with a story.\n\nOn Sunday Dorothy Lark walked the hedgerows with no case at all, just the sun and the birds and the good green smell of Willowmere village. A detective, she reflected, is only a neighbor who pays attention.\n\nConstable Henry Bly brought flowers — 'for the best detective in the county,' they said, gruff as ever. Dorothy Lark put them in water and told them not to be silly, but she kept the card."}]}

